  <h1>Lab Forms + Canvas</h1>

  <p>
    Every reflection question in the lessons (bug hunts, tinker challenges,
    socratic checkpoints) now has an answer box. Answers save on the
    student's device as they type, and each lesson has a
    <strong>Download answers (.json)</strong> button that produces one
    small, self-describing file — the question text, the student's answer,
    and a stable question id all travel together. This page is the whole
    loop for collecting, grading, and returning them through Canvas.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What students do</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the lesson (e.g. <a href="../lessons/1.1c.html">1.1c</a>).</li>
    <li>Type their name in the bar at the bottom of the lesson once — it is
      remembered on that device for every other lesson.</li>
    <li>Answer the boxed questions as they work through the labs. Everything
      autosaves locally; nothing is lost if they close the tab.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Download answers (.json)</strong> and upload that file
      to the Canvas assignment.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The Dashboard also has a <strong>My work</strong> panel: one file backs
    up <em>all</em> answers plus progress, and can be restored on another
    machine (useful when lab machines get reimaged).
  </p>

  <h2>2. Set up the Canvas assignment (once per lesson)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In Canvas: <strong>Assignments &rarr; + Assignment</strong>.</li>
    <li>Submission Type: <strong>Online</strong>, check
      <strong>File Uploads</strong>. (Leave "Allowed File Types" blank so
      .json files upload cleanly.)</li>
    <li>Set points and the due date, then Publish.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. Grade the whole class in one command</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the assignment in Canvas &rarr;
      <strong>Download Submissions</strong> (top right of the assignment
      page). Canvas gives you a zip; unzip it.</li>
    <li>In this repo:
      <pre><code>npm run grade -- path/to/unzipped-submissions -o grades.csv</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>You get:
      <ul>
        <li>a summary table in the terminal,</li>
        <li><code>grades.csv</code> — per student: answered/total and
          objective score,</li>
        <li><code>feedback/Student_Lesson.txt</code> — every question, the
          student's answer, and the expected answer for anything keyed.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Enter scores: open the Canvas gradebook and type them from the CSV,
      or paste the CSV into an exported gradebook sheet and re-import
      (Gradebook &rarr; Actions &rarr; Import).</li>
    <li>Return work: in SpeedGrader, drag the matching
      <code>feedback/*.txt</code> onto a student's submission comment, or
      attach the whole feedback folder if you use Canvas's bulk
      "Upload Grades" file return.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>4. How scoring works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every question</strong> is completeness-graded: answered or
      blank (minimum 2 characters). The default signal is the
      answered-percentage — a reasonable daily-work grade.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyed questions</strong> are also graded right/wrong. Keys
      live in <code>src/data/answerKeys.ts</code>. Listed questions:
      <pre><code>npm run dump-questions            # all 700+
npm run dump-questions -- 1.1c     # one lesson</code></pre>
      Copy an id into the keys file and list accepted answers — plain
      strings match as substrings ("integer division"), and
      <code>'re:...'</code> entries are regexes. Rebuild the site after
      editing keys (the grader reads the file at run time, so no rebuild is
      needed for grading — only the dump listing changes).</li>
    <li>Multiple attempts: the latest download per student per lesson is
      graded; earlier ones are ignored automatically.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Honest limits + future paths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>This is a static site — the download/upload step is the trade for
      having no student accounts or backend. It also means answers survive
      anything: they are plain JSON files you can read years later.</li>
    <li>Real-time push into Canvas (students click "submit" on the lesson
      page) would need a small server with a Canvas API token or an LTI
      tool registered with your Canvas instance. The CSV loop above gets
      you the same autograde-and-return outcome with one command per
      assignment, so start there.</li>
    <li>Code the students <em>write</em> in Discovery Labs is still .java
      files — those stay separate submissions (they are the program, not
      the reflection). The answer boxes capture the predict/explain work
      around the code.</li>
  </ul>

